 ## Overview
-Scene Derivatives for Evolving Surfaces is a mathematical framework for differentiating integrals over implicit surfaces that change shape with respect to scene parameters $\pi$ [S1][S2, unofficial]. It solves the problem of computing the gradient of rendering path integrals where the geometry is defined as a zero-level set $\phi(\mathbf{x}; \pi) = 0$, by decomposing the derivative into an interior term (accounting for field changes and surface curvature) and a boundary term (accounting for discontinuities) [S2, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from standard Reynolds transport formulations by explicitly handling the parameterization dependence of tangential velocity and providing a parameterization-independent "normal scene derivative" for implicit surfaces, building on [Reynolds Transport Theorem for Differentiable Rendering](6839-reynolds-transport-theorem-for-differentiable-rendering.md).
+Scene Derivatives for Evolving Surfaces is a mathematical framework for differentiating integrals over implicit surfaces that change shape with respect to scene parameters $\pi$ [S1][S2, unofficial]. It solves the problem of computing the gradient of rendering path integrals where the geometry is defined as a zero-level set $\phi(\mathbf{x}; \pi) = 0$, by decomposing the derivative into an interior term (accounting for field changes and surface curvature) and a boundary term (accounting for discontinuities) [S2, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from standard Reynolds transport formulations by explicitly handling the parameterization dependence of tangential velocity and providing a parameterization-independent "normal scene derivative" for implicit surfaces [S2, unofficial], building on [Reynolds Transport Theorem for Differentiable Rendering](6839-reynolds-transport-theorem-for-differentiable-rendering.md).
 
